        "15": {
          "id": "15",
          "name": "historic_style.md",
          "contents": "# Webpage Style Plan\n\n## Overall Mood\nHeroic, weathered, solemn, triumphant\n\nThe page should feel like standing at the edge of a frozen river on the eve of something historic — tense, courageous, and full of weight. It should not feel like a theme park attraction or a modern action movie site.\n\n## Visual Style\nUse a cinematic, broadsheet-inspired layout with aged textures and dramatic composition — think a museum exhibition crossed with a movie poster.\n\nThe design should help the audience feel the gravity of the moment and understand why this crossing was a turning point in American history.\n\n### Required colors\n- Page background: Deep navy (#0D1B2A) — like the dark pre-dawn sky over the Delaware River\n- Main headings: Aged parchment (#E8DFC0) — evokes colonial documents and candlelight\n- Accent color: Patriot red (#8B1A1A) — muted, not bright — worn and serious\n- Body text: Warm off-white (#D6CCAF)\n- Card or section backgrounds: Dark slate blue (#1B2A3B) with a subtle aged texture feel\n- Optional highlight color: Dull gold (#B8932A) — for buttons, dates, callouts, and decorative dividers\n\n### Avoid\n- Bright, saturated reds, whites, and blues (flag-waving cliché — too festive)\n- Rounded card corners or bubbly UI elements (too modern and playful)\n- Sans-serif-only headlines (loses the historic gravitas)\n- Drop shadows and gradients that look digital or neon\n- Anything that feels like a Fourth of July party\n\n## Typography\n\nUse simple, readable typography with a hint of historic authority.\n\n### Font style\n- Headings: A serif font such as Georgia or \"Palatino Linotype\" — stately, legible, and available without imports\n- Body text: A clean serif or transitional sans-serif for readability — Georgia or system-ui as fallback is appropriate\n- Do not use decorative, handwritten, or novelty fonts. The type should feel like a printed broadside from 1776, not a costume.\n\n### Headings\n- Headings should be bold, wide-tracked (slightly spaced letters), and easy to scan.\n- The page title should be large and commanding — this is a historic event, treat it that way.\n- Section headings should be smaller, with a dull gold rule or ornamental divider beneath them.\n- Use ALL CAPS sparingly for eyebrow labels (e.g., \"DECEMBER 25, 1776\") above major headings.\n\n### Body text\n- Body text should be easy to read against dark backgrounds.\n- Use comfortable line spacing (1.7–1.8).\n- Keep paragraphs short — 3 to 4 sentences max.\n- Use short lists when summarizing key facts or cast/crew.\n\n## Iconography\n\nUse simple inline SVG icons. Do not use external icon libraries, scripts, or imports such as Lucide, Font Awesome, Material Icons, or any other third-party icon set.\n\n### Suggested Icons\n- A simple rowing oar silhouette (horizontal, slightly angled) — for navigation or scene sections\n- A colonial tricorn hat outline (three-pointed hat shape, no fill, stroke only) — for character or cast sections\n- A single cannonball or musket shape (minimal, oval or long barrel) — for historical facts or timeline markers\n- A fleur-de-lis–style star (5-point, slightly ornate) — for rating or highlight callouts\n- A wax seal circle with a simple star or eagle inside — for official-looking section badges or pull quotes\n",
          "folderId": "4",
          "active": false
        },
        "16": {
          "id": "16",
          "name": "book_style.md",
          "contents": "# Webpage Style Plan\n\n## Overall Mood\nCurious, thoughtful, mysterious, reflective.\n\nThe page should feel intriguing and slightly unusual, like readers are entering Christopher’s way of seeing the world. It should feel intelligent, clean, and a little playful, while still leaving room for the mystery and emotional depth of the story. It should not feel childish, flashy, chaotic, or overly dark.\n\n## Visual Style\nUse a clean, modern book-profile style with strong headings, simple sections, and a few bold visual accents inspired by the cover.\n\nThe design should help the audience quickly understand what the book is about, notice the key themes, and feel interested in reading it. It should feel a little like a student-friendly Goodreads page, but more visually distinctive and connected to the cover design.\n\n### Required colors\n- Page background: warm off-white, light cream, or pale gray\n- Main headings: strong red inspired by the book cover\n- Accent color: muted charcoal, black, or soft gray\n- Body text: dark charcoal\n- Card or section backgrounds: white, very light gray, or pale warm gray\n- Optional highlight color: soft muted red or dusty rose for buttons, dividers, or small callouts\n\n### Avoid\n- Bright neon colors\n- A rainbow palette or too many competing accent colors\n- Overly cute or cartoonish design choices\n- Visual clutter that takes attention away from the book and its themes\n\n## Typography\n\nUse simple, readable typography.\n\n### Font style\n- Use a clean sans-serif font unless the style plan specifically calls for a different readable font.\n- If desired, headings can have a slightly condensed or bold look inspired by the book cover, but they should still be easy to read.\n- Do not use decorative, handwritten, or novelty fonts unless they clearly match the purpose and remain readable.\n\n### Headings\n- Headings should be bold and easy to scan.\n- Use clear size differences between the page title, section headings, and card headings.\n- The main title should feel prominent, similar to the strong typographic emphasis on the cover.\n\n### Body text\n- Body text should be easy to read.\n- Use comfortable line spacing.\n- Keep paragraphs short.\n- Use lists when they make information easier to scan.\n\n## Iconography\n\n### Suggested Icons\n- A simple magnifying glass or clue icon to represent the mystery\n- A simple dog outline or paw print shape as a nod to Wellington and the title\n- A simple math, pattern, or logic-inspired symbol such as dots, a grid, or connected lines\n",
          "folderId": "4",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [

